# m0-image.env — the single source of truth for every pinned value the M0
# base-image build depends on. The build script (build-m0-image.sh), the boot
# validator (boot-validate.sh), and the anti-drift guard (verify-image-pins.sh)
# all read THIS file; the README quotes the same values in prose and
# verify-image-pins.sh enforces that the README and this file agree
# (token-for-token), so a pin bump is one reviewed diff that cannot drift the
# README out from under it.
#
# This file is POSIX-sourced (KEY=value, no spaces around `=`); it is also
# grepped by verify-image-pins.sh, so keep one pin per line with the exact
# `M0_<NAME>=<value>` shape.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

# --- D49: the pinned Claude Code runtime version baked into the image ---
# The exact CC version this image — and the client/wrapper/adapters/claude-code
# adapter — target (D49). Bumping it is a deliberate, reviewed change paired
# with a goldentrace canary pass (client/goldentrace/README.md), never an
# `npm i -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code@latest`. This is the same pin the
# host-side drive-tier image (scripts/cc-sandbox/Containerfile) and the
# goldentrace e2e (client/goldentrace/e2e/live_drive.go) carry; the three move
# together.
M0_CC_VERSION=2.1.173

# --- doc 11 §3.2/§8.6: the glibc userland adjudication ---
# The guest userland is glibc (Debian), NOT musl/Alpine. Adjudicated in
# doc 11 §8.6: musl's stub silently ignores REFUSED, so an ask-posture lookup
# from a musl userland stalls the full 5 s ds-dnsgate resolver timeout then
# fails EAI_AGAIN; glibc fails fast (≤2 attempts). The golden image is glibc so
# that stall is confined to musl userlands the agent itself spawns, never the
# image's own resolver path. The base is digest-pinned the same way the
# drive-tier Containerfile pins node:22-bookworm-slim.
M0_BASE_DISTRO=debian
M0_BASE_SUITE=bookworm
# Node major baked for the npm-installed CC runtime (matches the drive-tier
# node:22-bookworm-slim base; CC ships a bundled node, but the npm global
# install path wants a host node/npm present at bake time).
M0_NODE_MAJOR=22

# --- D38: where the VM entrypoint binary is staged in the image ---
# The guest-side runtime/v1 implementation (vm/entrypoint/, README-reserved
# until the M0 freeze PR — proto/dreamserpent/runtime/v1/ is unfrozen, so the
# binary itself is a SEPARATE task and the build stages whatever artifact that
# task produces at this path). The image boot launches THIS path as the agent
# supervisor (launch/supervise/inject per the D38 contract); the VM-local event
# socket it opens terminates at the host agent (doc 15 §5.4). The image bakes
# the staging path + the boot wiring; it does not embed the contract.
M0_ENTRYPOINT_PATH=/usr/local/bin/ds-entrypoint
# The opaque entrypoint_config_ref (D38) is handed in at session create through
# SessionMaterial/VmSpec (doc 15 §5.1) — the image reserves the drop location,
# it does NOT bake a config (runtime ignorance, D20). This dir is ALSO the mount
# point of the per-session config-drive (below): the host attaches a read-only
# second disk holding config.pb and the run-ds-entrypoint.mount unit mounts it
# here, so ds-entrypoint's loadConfig finds config.pb.
M0_ENTRYPOINT_CONFIG_DIR=/run/ds/entrypoint

# --- D38 (gap-1): the per-session config-drive the host attaches ---
# The host agent delivers the structured runtimev1.EntrypointConfig into the guest
# as a per-session READ-ONLY config-drive (a second disk), built host-side by the
# libvirt driver's EntrypointDeliverer (orchestrator/internal/hypervisor/libvirt/
# configdrive.go). These MUST match that producer (replicated here, never imported
# — D80 keeps the guest tree from crossing into orchestrator/): the volume label
# (configDriveVolumeLabel) the guest mount unit finds the drive by, and the
# filesystem (iso9660 — genisoimage -rational-rock -joliet, read-only by
# construction). The run-ds-entrypoint.mount unit mounts /dev/disk/by-label/<label>
# at M0_ENTRYPOINT_CONFIG_DIR read-only.
M0_CONFIG_DRIVE_LABEL=DS_ENTRYPOINT
M0_CONFIG_DRIVE_FS=iso9660

# --- U4: the per-session GUEST static net config (second config-drive file) ---
# For the VM to egress over the ROUTED TAP (the nft4 keystone), the guest must
# address its tap NIC with the static per-session address + default route. The host
# renders those L3 facts (10.77.<idx>.1/31 via 10.77.<idx>.0) into a SECOND file on
# the SAME per-session config-drive, named M0_NETCFG_FILE, alongside config.pb —
# emitted ONLY when the host runs the routed tap (orchestrator/internal/hypervisor/
# libvirt/netconfig.go gated on LiveConfig.RoutedTap). The guest unit ds-netcfg.service
# runs M0_NETCFG_SCRIPT_PATH (ds-apply-netcfg.sh) early, Before=ds-entrypoint.service:
# it NO-OPS when the file is absent (the SLIRP/offline path is byte-identical) and
# applies ip addr/route when present. M0_NETCFG_FILE MUST match the host producer's
# file name (netConfigFileName), replicated here, NEVER imported (D80).
M0_NETCFG_SCRIPT_PATH=/usr/local/bin/ds-apply-netcfg
M0_NETCFG_FILE=ds-net.env

# --- SLIRP/dev egress DHCP (fixes the no-IP/no-DNS SLIRP hang) ---
# On the M0-minimal usermode SLIRP path (the default, and every synthetic/offline
# boot) the NIC has NO static config and NO ds-net.env: without a DHCP client the
# guest gets no IP/DNS and CC loops on api_retry forever (live-found 2026-06-18).
# The fix bakes a systemd-networkd DHCP .network for the SLIRP NIC, but it must NOT
# be placed where networkd loads it at boot — the routed production tap shares the
# NIC names (eth0 under net.ifnames=0, en* under default naming) and would be
# DHCP-raced against ds-netcfg's static apply. So the .network is STAGED at this
# NON-search path and installed into /run/systemd/network ONLY on the SLIRP path
# (ds-slirp-net.service, gated ConditionPathExists=!<config-dir>/M0_NETCFG_FILE).
# On a routed-tap boot the file is never loaded, so its [Match] provably cannot
# catch the tap; ds-netcfg.service owns that NIC there.
M0_SLIRP_NETWORK_STAGE=/usr/local/share/ds/ds-slirp-dhcp.network

# --- gap-3: the guest-side attach carriage forwarder (vm/attachfwd) ---
# ds-attachfwd LISTENs on the guest-local attach event-socket UDS (which
# ds-entrypoint emits CC stdio onto, AttachWiring.event_socket_path) and on a TCP
# carriage the host-agent's per-session attach bridge dials over the tap at
# guestIP:M0_ATTACH_PORT (orchestrator/internal/hostagent/attachbridge.go execs
# ds-hostbridge --guest-attach-addr guestIP:4242). It is a 1:1 byte splice; the
# D61 fan-out / token-auth / writer-seat live host-side. The image stages the
# binary + the boot unit (ds-attachfwd.service, ordered Before ds-entrypoint); the
# host<->guest :M0_ATTACH_PORT tap NFT allow is nft4's (a DECLARED dependency, NOT
# written by this image). M0_ATTACH_PORT MUST match the host-side
# libvirt.DefaultAttachPort (4242) the bridge dials.
M0_ATTACHFWD_PATH=/usr/local/bin/ds-attachfwd
M0_ATTACHFWD_UDS_PATH=/run/ds/attach.sock
M0_ATTACH_PORT=4242

# --- raw disk geometry (D29: raw base under the per-session qcow2 overlay) ---
# The base is a RAW image at rest (D29: "raw golden image + per-session qcow2
# overlay"); we build into a qcow2 only as the convenient hand-build/boot-test
# container and convert/treat it as the raw base on the M0 host. Size is the
# sparse virtual size; the on-disk file stays small (never committed regardless,
# see README — blobs live under ~/tmp/ds-images/, never the repo).
M0_DISK_VIRTUAL_SIZE=12G

# --- terminal/PTY mode (doc serpent-cli-mvp 02 §2.9 / 10 §A2): the TERM the baked
# terminfo supports ---
# The TERM value the in-guest pty launch-mode of ds-entrypoint sets for the
# interactive CC TUI. ds-entrypoint selects PIPES vs PTY stdio per-session from the
# host-resolved LaunchSpec (no image change between modes); in PTY mode the host
# passes TERM=<this> via LaunchSpec.env (a host-resolved launch fact, NOT a guest
# CC-ism). This pin is documentation+enforcement, NOT a behavior knob: it asserts
# the baked image actually CARRIES this TERM's terminfo entry so a garbled-TUI
# regression (missing terminfo) fails the bake, not the live session (doc 02 R4).
# The terminfo entry MUST exist in the image at /lib/terminfo/<c>/<TERM> (where
# ncurses-base on Debian bookworm installs it — confirmed present:
# /lib/terminfo/x/xterm-256color); build-m0-image.sh asserts it at bake, and
# verify-image-pins.sh enforces README<->env agreement on this pin.
M0_PTY_TERM=xterm-256color

# --- terminal/PTY mode: the COLORTERM the launch-mode advertises (24-bit color) ---
# The COLORTERM value the in-guest pty launch-mode of ds-entrypoint advertises to CC's
# color detection, alongside M0_PTY_TERM. Like TERM, it is NOT baked into the image: the
# host passes COLORTERM=<this> via LaunchSpec.env (a host-resolved launch fact), and only
# when the facts carry no COLORTERM of their own (host override wins). Unlike TERM this
# value has NO terminfo dependency — "truecolor" is a free-standing capability hint CC's
# palette detection reads — so there is NO bake-time terminfo assert and NO README quote;
# this pin exists purely to hold the host-side const in lockstep with the env, mirroring
# sessionmode.go's DefaultTerminalCOLORTERM: the two MUST move together, or a COLORTERM
# bump silently changes the in-VM color palette while TERM stays pinned. verify-image-
# pins.sh's (1c) lockstep check enforces that. (No README<->env quote check: COLORTERM is
# not surfaced as a user-facing prose pin the way M0_PTY_TERM/M0_CC_VERSION are.)
M0_PTY_COLORTERM=truecolor

# --- D75: guest-interior IPv6 posture (see guest-config/99-ds-disable-ipv6.conf) ---
# v0 guest is v4-only: the golden image disables v6 via per-interface sysctl on
# EGRESS NICs only; `lo` keeps ::1; kernel `ipv6.disable=1` is FORBIDDEN (Docker
# 28.x breakage, D75). The egress NIC glob the sysctl drop-in targets:
M0_EGRESS_NIC_GLOB=en*

# --- dogfood: the per-session WORKSPACE disk (01KWHCG6EV) ---------------------
# The workspace is delivered as a THIRD disk, not cloned in-guest. This is forced,
# not preferred: the workspace repo is generally PRIVATE (an SSH/HTTPS forge
# remote), so an in-guest `git clone` would need a forge credential
# inside the VM — exactly what D22 forbids ("long-lived credentials never enter
# the VM"). The host already holds both the repo and the credential, so the host
# materializes the workspace and hands the guest a filesystem instead.
#
# Unlike the config-drive this disk is READ-WRITE (the agent edits code on it) and
# it is the agent's ONLY writable surface that the host reads back deliberately —
# the host mounts it after the session to review the diff, which is the same
# review posture as the D29 COW overlay delta but scoped to the workspace.
#
# CONTRACT MATCH (scripts/host-bringup/ds-make-workspace.sh builds the disk; the
# libvirt driver attaches it as vdc): the guest finds it by LABEL, never by device
# node, exactly as the config-drive does. ext4 because the guest must write it.
M0_WORKSPACE_LABEL=DS_WORKSPACE
M0_WORKSPACE_DIR=/work
M0_WORKSPACE_FS=ext4

# --- dogfood: the baked dev toolchains (01KWHCG6EV scope 2) -------------------
# Baked into the image rather than fetched at session time, and that ordering is
# deliberate: the POL-2 egress baseline allowlists the module/crate PROTOCOL
# endpoints (proxy.golang.org, crates.io, ...) but NOT the toolchain installers
# (go.dev/dl, static.rust-lang.org, sh.rustup.rs), because an agent that can fetch
# and execute a new toolchain is a materially larger surface than one that can
# only fetch source dependencies. A gated session therefore CANNOT install a
# toolchain; it must already have one.
#
# Baking a compiler is NOT a boundary widening the way an allowlist row is: an
# egress row grants every session new reach to the internet, whereas a compiler
# only lets an agent that already has arbitrary code execution (by design) turn
# source into binaries. The cost is image size, not blast radius.
#
# M0_RUST_VERSION MUST equal dataplane/rust-toolchain.toml's pinned channel. If it
# drifts, cargo honours the repo's rust-toolchain.toml, tries to fetch the pinned
# channel from static.rust-lang.org, and DIES at the gate — a confusing failure
# that looks like a network bug and is actually a pin mismatch. verify-image-pins.sh
# enforces the lockstep.
# M0_GO_VERSION must satisfy the highest `go` directive across the repo's modules
# (go 1.25.11 at the time of writing).
#
# The SHA-256 of each release tarball is pinned alongside its version and VERIFIED
# in the bake before the archive is unpacked. This is the highest-leverage integrity
# check in the image: this compiler builds the code the agent then runs, so a
# substituted toolchain compromises everything downstream of it, and the bake pulls
# these over the host's TLS-intercepting egress gateway (so "we used HTTPS" is not
# on its own an argument about what bytes arrived). Bump version and hash together.
#   go:   https://go.dev/dl/?mode=json&include=all  -> files[].sha256
#   rust: https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/<tarball>.sha256
M0_GO_VERSION=1.26.5
M0_GO_SHA256=5c2c3b16caefa1d968a94c1daca04a7ca301a496d9b086e17ad77bb81393f053
M0_GO_PREFIX=/usr/local/go
M0_RUST_VERSION=1.95.0
M0_RUST_SHA256=a47ac940abd12399d59ad15c877e7113fa35f2b9ec7e6a8a045d4fd8b9741dea
M0_RUST_PREFIX=/opt/rust
